{#- Générateur synthétique du cas d'usage « prévision de consommation électrique régionale » (famille time_series_forecasting). -#}
"""Synthetic data generator — {{ spec.data.title }}.

Business scenario
    {{ wrap(business.context, 96, indent=4) }}

How the data is built
    The generator does **not** draw rows independently: it first simulates a daily electricity
    load series with an explicit, physically motivated model, then expands that series into a
    supervised frame of ``(origin, horizon)`` pairs.

    Step 1 — the underlying daily series (~3.4 years):

    * **base level and slow trend** — regional consumption around {{ extras.get('data_conf', {}).get('base_load_mw', 2100) }} MW with a mild
      efficiency-driven decline (~-0.8 % per year);
    * **annual seasonality** — winter peak, summer trough (sinusoid on the day of year);
    * **weekly seasonality** — Monday ramp, Friday evening drop, deep weekend trough;
    * **thermo-sensitivity** — the dominant French driver: +3.5 % of load per degree below 15 °C
      (heating) and +2.2 % per degree above 24 °C (cooling), applied on a two-day smoothed
      temperature to reproduce building inertia;
    * **calendar** — the eleven metropolitan public holidays (Easter-based dates computed with the
      Meeus algorithm, not hard-coded per year), plus ``pont`` bridge days and school holidays;
    * **regime breaks** — two cold snaps, two heatwaves and three industrial shutdowns, i.e. the
      days where a forecast is worth money;
    * **autocorrelated noise** — multiplicative AR(1) with rho ~0.6, so residuals are *not* white
      and naive Gaussian intervals are too narrow.

    Step 2 — the supervised expansion. For every origin day ``t`` and every horizon
    ``h in (1, 2, 3, 7)`` a row is emitted carrying the load observed at ``t + h``.

    The anteriority contract (the single most important property of this dataset)
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Every feature falls in exactly one of three buckets:

    * ``known at origin`` — ``load_last_observed``, ``load_lag_*``, ``load_rolling_*``,
      ``temperature_lag_1d``, ``temperature_rolling_mean_7d``: computed on the series **up to and
      including ``t``**;
    * ``known in advance`` — the calendar of the target day (weekday, weekend, public holiday,
      school holiday, day-of-year encoding) and the **temperature forecast** for that day *and for
      the day before it*, which is the truth corrupted by a forecast error growing with the horizon
      (0.35 °C at J+1 up to ~1.2 °C at J+7). The day-before forecast matters because the load reacts
      to a two-day smoothed temperature: without it no model can rebuild the building inertia. Real forecasters never get the true future temperature, and pretending
      otherwise is the classic leakage that makes a backtest look brilliant;
    * ``ground truth only`` — ``load_mw`` (the target), ``target_date``, ``target_month``,
      ``event_type`` and ``is_extreme_event``: metadata used to measure and to explain errors,
      excluded from the feature matrix by ``drop_columns``.

    ``load_seasonal_naive`` deserves a note: it reads the load of ``target_date - 7 days``, which
    looks like it reaches into the future but never does, because every horizon is <= 7 days, so
    ``t + h - 7 <= t``. It is both a legitimate feature (the seasonal-naive anchor) and the
    reference the model must beat.

    Two traps keep the exercise honest: informative missingness on the observed temperature
    (~3 %, winter-biased — sensors fail in freezing weather, which is exactly when the load is
    most temperature-sensitive) and irreducible noise, so a MAPE near zero would mean leakage, not
    a good model.

Determinism
    ``generate()`` is a pure function of ``(n_samples, seed, options)``: two runs produce the same
    bytes, which is what makes stack-to-stack comparisons meaningful.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

from collections.abc import Mapping, Sequence
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any, ClassVar

import numpy as np
